Lets the core refuse a page what is the user's device's or person's to give, and end a question
the browser would put to the user for it, which it does for a page in an agent's Space. Hooks only:
each is one null check where the core has set none, and which page is refused what is the core's
(//browser_core/agent_permission_rule.h).

components/permissions/permission_context_base.cc. PermissionContextBase::GetPermissionStatus asks
g_browser_core_no_permission for the frame and the kind after the kill switch and before any saved
setting is read, and answers DENIED from ACTOR_OVERRIDE, the answer Chrome gives a page its own
actor works on, so every reader of a status and every request sees one answer. RequestPermission's
ACTOR_OVERRIDE case tells g_browser_core_permission_refused, so the core hears of a request
refused so and never of a read.

components/permissions/permission_request_manager.cc. PermissionRequestManager::AddRequest asks
g_browser_core_takes_permission_request first, with the frame and the request. One the core takes
ends with the call: the manager owns the request and frees it as it returns, before it is queued,
shown, counted or recorded.

chrome/browser/media/webrtc/media_capture_devices_dispatcher.cc.
MediaCaptureDevicesDispatcher::ProcessMediaAccessRequest asks g_browser_core_no_media_access first,
before any handler has the request and so before any picker, and answers a refused one
PERMISSION_DENIED with no devices.

chrome/browser/printing/print_view_manager.cc.
PrintViewManager::RejectPrintPreviewRequestIfRestricted, which every print preview passes before
its dialog is made (a page's own print call, its PDF viewer's button, the browser's Print command),
asks g_browser_core_no_print for the frame, saying whether the browser's own command started it.
One the core refuses takes the road Chrome has for a restricted preview: the print ends and its
page goes on.

chrome/browser/download/chrome_download_manager_delegate.cc.
ChromeDownloadManagerDelegate::RequestConfirmation, which Chrome asks before it puts a sheet up
for where a download's file goes, asks g_browser_core_takes_download_question first, with the
download, the path Chrome suggested, why it asks and the callback. One the core takes it has
answered through the callback, and no sheet is made.

content/browser/file_system_access/file_system_access_handle_base.cc. The constructor
FileSystemAccessHandleBase::FileSystemAccessHandleBase, which every handle on a file or a folder
runs, asks g_browser_core_no_file_handle for its frame, saying whether the handle is in the site's
own storage. One the core refuses keeps fixed denied grants in place of those it came with, under
the same path and name, as a handle in a third-party context does: every answer about a handle
reads what it keeps, so each is a denial and nothing asks the user. The pointer is exported, as
the clipboard's is.

content/browser/renderer_host/clipboard_host_impl.cc. FrameClipboardContext::CanWrite asks
g_browser_core_no_clipboard_write for its frame, where every write of a page's clipboard host is
already asked whether its context may write; a refused write is dropped as one from a context that
may not write is. The pointer is exported, since content is a library of its own in a component
build and the core is outside it.

